  "final_text": "All four steps are done on the local Kibana 9.5.5. The rule reads back as enabled.\n\n- **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (`kibana_sample_data_flights`) has 27 fields, including `Carrier`, `Cancelled`, `DestCityName`, `DistanceKilometers` and `FlightDelayMin`. Its time field is `timestamp`.\n- **Dashboard:** 'S4-fb57b319 Flights' (`f594a29b-a8ac-47be-84f4-c23fac239d32`) has two panels, both on the flights data view by reference:\n  - 'Flights over time' is a line chart of count by `timestamp` date histogram.\n  - The carrier panel is a bar chart of count by `Carrier` terms, limit 10.\n- **Alert rule:** 'S4-fb57b319 Cancelled flights' (`4ce16f3d-bc7d-4583-9726-a30b89b2eb78`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on the flights data view. It counts documents matching `Cancelled : true` over a 5-minute window on `timestamp`, fires when the count is above 10, and runs every 1m. It was created enabled, so no enable call was needed.\n- **Carrier panel rename:** the panel was first created as 'Flights by carrier (initial)' because I added the rename as a separate step. It is now 'Flights by carrier', and the dashboard still has two panels.\n\nChoices I made that you may want to change: the rule uses the `alerts` consumer, the carrier chart is vertical bars, and the over-time chart is a line.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
